From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:27:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 162/176] fix(gastown): make test_pr_artifact_lifecycle's
 fixture TMP physical on darwin (gcp-xspkl)

test_pr_artifact_lifecycle.sh took TMP straight from mktemp -d. On macOS
that is /var/folders/..., and /var is a symlink to /private/var, so the
artifact_dir written to the fake bead was a non-physical path. The cleanup
script canonicalises its city and rig namespace with pwd -P, and its
missing-path branch cannot realpath an artifact that is already removed, so
task_artifact_path_shape compared the raw /var/... string against the
/private/var namespace and rejected it: ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_BLOCKED ... unsafe
layout for it-1, exit 1. Linux CI passes because /tmp is physical there.

The fixture was at fault, not the script. Production writes artifact_dir from
validate_artifact_worktree, which returns the pwd -P form, so the pwd -P
strictness in the cleanup script is the safety property and stays untouched.
The fix resolves TMP physically right after mktemp, as
test_polecat_artifact_dir.sh and test_shutdown_progress_lane_homes.sh already do.

Validation (darwin, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null): the test was rc=1 with the
unsafe-layout line before the change. After it, rc=0 with the default TMPDIR,
with TMPDIR=/tmp, and with an already-physical TMPDIR. Deleting only the new
line reproduces rc=1 and the same line.
---
 gastown/tests/test_pr_artifact_lifecycle.sh | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/gastown/tests/test_pr_artifact_lifecycle.sh b/gastown/tests/test_pr_artifact_lifecycle.sh
index 6514face..508a1d28 100755
--- a/gastown/tests/test_pr_artifact_lifecycle.sh
+++ b/gastown/tests/test_pr_artifact_lifecycle.sh
@@ -6,6 +6,11 @@ RECONCILE="$ROOT/gastown/commands/pr-merge-reconcile/run.sh"
 CLEANUP="$ROOT/gastown/assets/scripts/task-artifact-cleanup.sh"
 REAL_GIT=$(command -v git)
 TMP=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/gascity-pr-artifact-test.XXXXXX")
+# mktemp -d returns /var/folders/... on macOS, where /var is a symlink to
+# /private/var. Production writes artifact_dir in its pwd -P form, and the
+# cleanup script cannot resolve an artifact path that is already removed, so
+# the fixture must start physical too (gcp-xspkl).
+TMP=$(cd "$TMP" && pwd -P)
 trap 'rm -rf "$TMP"' EXIT
 
 BIN="$TMP/bin"
